\title{Degree-Based Spilling Is Not Optimal\\on Chordal Interference Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 29, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Register allocation models simultaneously live program values as adjacent vertices of an interference graph and physical registers as colors. This model suggests two attractive but incorrect degree-based principles: that the register requirement should equal $\max\{\Delta(G)+1,\omega(G)\}$, and that spilling a maximum-degree vertex should optimally reduce register pressure. We give a self-contained eight-vertex counterexample to both claims. The graph is the disjoint union $K_3\sqcup K_{1,4}$ of a triangle and a four-leaf star. It is chordal, as witnessed by an explicit perfect elimination ordering. Its maximum degree is $4$, its clique number and chromatic number are both $3$, and it is $3$-colorable despite the proposed value $\Delta(G)+1=5$. Under a two-register budget, deleting the unique maximum-degree vertex leaves the triangle and therefore fails, whereas deleting a degree-$2$ triangle vertex yields a bipartite graph and succeeds. The example shows that local edge incidence does not identify global coloring obstructions, even in structured interference graphs. We formulate the appropriate chordal-graph perspective, describe reproducible algorithms, and discuss clique-sensitive and weighted alternatives.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Register allocation assigns program values to a limited set of processor registers. Two values whose live ranges overlap cannot occupy the same register: one assignment would destroy a value still needed later. This constraint is represented by an undirected \emph{interference graph}, whose vertices are values and whose edges join pairs that are simultaneously live. Assigning registers is proper vertex coloring.

This translation separates two questions. First, can all values remain in registers under a budget of $k$ registers? Second, if not, which values should be spilled to memory so that the remaining graph becomes $k$-colorable? Both invite degree-based heuristics. A high-degree vertex conflicts with many others, while the universal greedy bound $\chi(G)\leq\Delta(G)+1$ relates maximum degree to colorability.

These facts can be overextended. One proposed exact formula is
\[
  \chi(G)=\max\{\Delta(G)+1,\omega(G)\},
\]
where $\omega(G)$ is the largest clique size. Another proposed rule is to spill a maximum-degree vertex. The first confuses an upper bound with an equality; the second confuses removal of many edges with removal of the obstruction that exceeds the register budget.

The distinction remains important for static single assignment (SSA) programs, whose interference graphs are commonly studied through chordal structure. Chordal graphs admit perfect elimination orderings and satisfy $\chi(G)=\omega(G)$. One might nevertheless wonder whether degree-based spilling becomes reliable in this class. It does not.

We establish this with an eight-vertex graph. One component is a triangle; the other is a star with four leaves. The star center has unique maximum degree but is irrelevant to the triangle that forces three colors. Spilling the center fails to achieve two-colorability, whereas spilling any triangle vertex succeeds. The same construction separates chromatic number $3$ from the degree-based value $5$.

\section{Graph-theoretic model}

\begin{definition}[Coloring and chromatic number]
Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple undirected graph. A proper $k$-coloring is a map
\[
 c:V\longrightarrow\{0,1,\ldots,k-1\}
\]
such that $c(u)\ne c(v)$ whenever $\{u,v\}\in E$. The least positive $k$ admitting such a map is the chromatic number $\chi(G)$.
\end{definition}

In the interference model, $\chi(G)$ is the least number of registers needed without spilling. For $v\in V$, let $N(v)$ be its neighborhood and $\deg(v)=|N(v)|$ its degree. The maximum degree is
\[
 \Delta(G)=\max_{v\in V}\deg(v).
\]
A clique is a set of pairwise adjacent vertices, and the maximum clique size is $\omega(G)$. Since clique members need distinct colors,
\[
 \omega(G)\leq\chi(G).
\]
Greedy coloring gives
\[
 \chi(G)\leq\Delta(G)+1.
\]
Indeed, when a vertex is colored, at most $\Delta(G)$ colors occur among its colored neighbors. This proves sufficiency, not necessity.

\begin{definition}[Spilling]
For a set $S\subseteq V$, the surviving graph after spilling $S$ is the induced subgraph $G-S$ on $V\setminus S$. We say that $G$ is $k$-colorable except for $S$ when $G-S$ is $k$-colorable.
\end{definition}

Equivalently, there is a map on all vertices such that every edge with both endpoints outside $S$ has differently colored endpoints. With spill cost $w(v)>0$, the general optimization problem is to minimize
\[
 \sum_{v\in S}w(v) \qquad\text{subject to}\qquad \chi(G-S)\leq k.
\]
The one-vertex comparison below isolates whether maximum degree identifies a successful choice.

\begin{definition}[Chordality and elimination]
A graph is chordal if every cycle of length at least four has an edge joining two nonconsecutive cycle vertices. An ordering $v_1,\ldots,v_n$ is a perfect elimination ordering in the earlier-neighbor convention if
\[
 N^-(v_i)=\{v_j:j<i\text{ and }\{v_i,v_j\}\in E\}
\]
is a clique for every $i$.
\end{definition}

A finite graph is chordal exactly when it has a perfect elimination ordering. Chordal graphs are perfect: every induced subgraph $H$ satisfies $\chi(H)=\omega(H)$. Thus clique number, not maximum degree, gives the exact register requirement in this class.

\section{The counterexample}

Let
\[
 V=\{0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7\}
\]
and
\[
 E=\bigl\{\{0,1\},\{0,2\},\{1,2\},
 \{3,4\},\{3,5\},\{3,6\},\{3,7\}\bigr\}.
\]
Thus
\[
 G=K_3\sqcup K_{1,4}.
\]
The graph has eight vertices and seven edges. Its disconnected form cleanly separates a component with high degree from one with high coloring demand.

\begin{theorem}[Perfect elimination]
The natural ordering $0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7$ is a perfect elimination ordering of $G$. Consequently, $G$ is chordal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The earlier-neighbor sets are
\begin{align*}
N^-(0)&=\varnothing, & N^-(1)&=\{0\}, & N^-(2)&=\{0,1\},\\
N^-(3)&=\varnothing, & N^-(4)&=\{3\}, & N^-(5)&=\{3\},\\
N^-(6)&=\{3\}, & N^-(7)&=\{3\}.&&
\end{align*}
Every empty set and singleton is a clique, and $\{0,1\}$ is a clique because $0$ and $1$ are adjacent. Hence the ordering is perfect, and the elimination characterization implies chordality.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Degree profile]
The degrees are
\[
\deg(0)=\deg(1)=\deg(2)=2,\qquad
\deg(3)=4,\qquad
\deg(4)=\cdots=\deg(7)=1.
\]
In particular, $\Delta(G)=4$, and vertex $3$ is the unique maximum-degree vertex.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Each triangle vertex is adjacent to the other two. The star center is adjacent to four leaves, and each leaf is adjacent only to the center. There are no edges between components.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Failure of the degree formula]
The graph $G$ satisfies
\[
 \omega(G)=\chi(G)=3,\qquad \Delta(G)+1=5.
\]
Consequently,
\[
 \chi(G)\ne\max\{\Delta(G)+1,\omega(G)\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Vertices $0,1,2$ form a clique, so $\omega(G)\geq3$ and $\chi(G)\geq3$. No clique crosses the disconnected components; the star has clique number $2$ and the triangle has clique number $3$. Hence $\omega(G)=3$.

For the upper bound, give $0,1,2$ colors $0,1,2$, respectively. Give the center $3$ color $0$ and every leaf color $1$. This is a proper $3$-coloring, so $\chi(G)=3$. The degree lemma gives $\Delta(G)+1=5$, and the displayed inequality follows.
\end{proof}

The defect in the proposed equality is structural. A high-degree vertex can have an independent neighborhood. The star's four leaves can all share one color. Degree counts constraints incident to a vertex but not dependencies among those constraints.

\section{Failure of maximum-degree spilling}

Fix a budget of two registers. The triangle prevents a two-coloring before any spill. We compare the maximum-degree candidate $3$ with the lower-degree candidate $0$.

\begin{lemma}[The maximum-degree spill fails]
The graph $G-3$ is not $2$-colorable.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Deleting $3$ leaves the triangle on $0,1,2$ unchanged. In a two-coloring, $0$ must differ from both $1$ and $2$. With only two colors, $1$ and $2$ must then have the same color, contradicting their adjacency.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[A lower-degree spill succeeds]
The graph $G-0$ is $2$-colorable.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
After deleting $0$, the triangle becomes the edge $\{1,2\}$. Assign color $0$ to $1$ and $3$, and color $1$ to $2,4,5,6,7$. The edge $\{1,2\}$ is proper, and every star edge joins $3$ to a leaf of the opposite color.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Maximum-degree spilling is not optimal]
There exists a chordal graph $G$ and vertices $x,y$ such that $x$ has maximum degree, $\deg(y)<\deg(x)$, $G-x$ is not $2$-colorable, and $G-y$ is $2$-colorable. For $G=K_3\sqcup K_{1,4}$, one may take $x=3$ and $y=0$, with $\deg(x)=4$ and $\deg(y)=2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Chordality follows from the perfect elimination theorem. The degree profile gives the strict degree comparison and maximum-degree status of $3$. The two preceding lemmas give failure after deleting $3$ and success after deleting $0$.
\end{proof}

Deleting $3$ removes four edges but leaves chromatic number $3$; deleting $0$ removes only two edges but lowers chromatic number to $2$. Thus maximizing deleted incident edges can be strictly worse than targeting the obstruction.

\section{Algorithms and reproducible checks}

\subsection{Chromatic number by backtracking}
For a small graph, order vertices by nonincreasing degree. For $k=1,2,\ldots$, recursively assign one of $k$ colors, rejecting a partial assignment as soon as adjacent vertices receive equal colors. The first feasible $k$ is $\chi(G)$. For fixed $k$ and $n=|V|$, the worst case examines $O(k^n)$ assignments. Applied here, the search rejects $k=1,2$ and accepts $k=3$.

\subsection{Checking the elimination order}
Given an ordering, compute every earlier-neighbor set and test all pairs in each set for adjacency. A direct adjacency-matrix implementation costs $O(n^3)$; specialized chordality algorithms run in $O(n+m)$. For the natural order, the only earlier-neighbor set of size greater than one is $\{0,1\}$ for vertex $2$, and its members are adjacent.

\subsection{Evaluating one-vertex spills}
For each candidate $v$, delete $v$ and test $k$-colorability. At $k=2$, breadth-first bipartiteness testing is linear in $n+m$, so testing every candidate independently costs $O(n(n+m))$. Deleting any of $0,1,2$ succeeds because it breaks the triangle. Deleting any of $3,4,5,6,7$ fails because the triangle remains.

\section{Implications for allocation}

\subsection{The corrected exact target}
For arbitrary graphs, $\Delta(G)+1$ is an upper bound, not an exact count. For chordal graphs the exact relation is
\[
 \chi(G)=\omega(G).
\]
This equality is constructive. Color vertices in the reverse of a perfect elimination ordering. When a vertex is reached, its already colored neighbors form a clique and have distinct colors. Assign the smallest absent color. The procedure uses no more colors than the largest clique, and no coloring can use fewer.

Empirical studies of SSA interference should therefore compare chromatic and clique numbers, recording maximum degree separately as a coarse bound.

\subsection{Clique lower bounds for spilling}

\begin{proposition}[Clique spill bound]
Let $C$ be a clique of size $m>k$. Every spill set $S$ for which $G-S$ is $k$-colorable satisfies
\[
 |S\cap C|\geq m-k.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
At most $k$ members of $C$ can survive because surviving clique vertices require distinct colors. Therefore at least $m-k$ members must be deleted.
\end{proof}

In the example, $C=\{0,1,2\}$, $m=3$, and $k=2$. Every successful spill set must contain a triangle vertex. The maximum-degree vertex lies outside $C$, so deleting it cannot meet this necessary condition. For chordal graphs, cliques determine chromatic number, making oversized cliques the central obstructions. Spill selection becomes a constrained hitting problem: remove enough vertices from every clique larger than $k$, while minimizing cost.

\subsection{Weighted costs}
Real spill costs differ. A value in a hot loop may be expensive to reload, while a constant may be cheap to reconstruct. Degree cannot see these weights. A practical score may combine degree, execution frequency, live-range length, rematerialization cost, and clique participation. Our theorem rules out universal optimality of pure maximum-degree choice; it does not rule out degree as one useful feature.

\subsection{Scope}
The disconnected construction is deliberately transparent. Chromatic number and maximum degree of a disjoint union are maxima over components, allowing different components to control the two statistics. Interference graphs need not be connected, so the scenario is operationally valid. Finding minimal connected chordal counterexamples and identifying subclasses with safe degree rules remain worthwhile refinements.

\section{Further algorithmic perspective}

The small example permits exhaustive computation, but the methods illustrate distinct algorithmic regimes. Exact coloring by unrestricted backtracking is exponential in the worst case because a partial assignment may branch over every available color. Degree ordering is a useful search heuristic: constrained vertices are considered early, so contradictions often arise before many choices have accumulated. It does not change the worst-case bound, and it should not be confused with a correctness theorem based on degree.

Chordal structure offers a stronger alternative. Given a perfect elimination ordering, reverse-order greedy coloring is optimal. At the moment a vertex is colored, its already colored neighbors are precisely a clique inherited from the elimination property. They therefore occupy distinct colors, and the smallest absent color is at most one more than their number. The largest color count created in this process is bounded by the size of a clique consisting of a vertex and its relevant neighbors. Since that clique also supplies a lower bound, the resulting coloring uses exactly $\omega(G)$ colors.

This reasoning separates recognition, coloring, and spilling. Recognition asks whether a suitable elimination order exists. Coloring uses the order to solve the unspilled register assignment. Spilling modifies the graph to satisfy a smaller budget and may require a genuinely different optimization method. An efficient solution to coloring does not make the vertex-deletion problem trivial.

For two registers, the spill experiment simplifies because two-colorability is equivalent to bipartiteness. A breadth-first or depth-first traversal alternates colors along edges and detects any edge joining equal-colored endpoints. Repeating this test after each possible one-vertex deletion produces a complete spill table in $O(n(n+m))$ time. In the example the successful set is exactly $\{0,1,2\}$, while $\{3,4,5,6,7\}$ is the failing set.

For larger budgets, exact evaluation may use backtracking, dynamic programming on chordal representations, or integer optimization. A standard binary model introduces a variable indicating whether each vertex is spilled and assignment variables for the surviving colors. Edge constraints prohibit equal colors at adjacent surviving vertices. Weighted objectives then minimize reload or recomputation cost rather than the number of removed vertices. Clique inequalities strengthen such a model by requiring enough deletions from every oversized clique.

\section{Compiler interpretation}

The graph model abstracts away instruction placement and runtime frequency, but its conclusion is operationally clear. The star center represents a value simultaneously live with four values that are never live with one another. Those four leaves may all reuse one register. The triangle represents three values pairwise live at the same time, so it creates irreducible three-register pressure. Counting conflicts treats these situations in the opposite order from their importance under a two-register budget.

A production spill score usually needs several signals. Dynamic execution frequency estimates how often loads and stores would run. Loop nesting emphasizes spills inside hot repeated regions. Rematerialization recognizes values that can be recomputed more cheaply than reloaded. Live-range splitting may avoid spilling a value everywhere. Graph structure contributes another signal: membership in cliques or other obstructions exceeding the current budget. Degree can remain among these features, but the theorem shows why it cannot be the sole universal criterion.

The corrected empirical question for chordal interference graphs is therefore whether the observed register demand agrees with clique number, as perfectness predicts. Maximum degree should be recorded independently as a coarse measure of local conflict. For spill studies, candidate quality should be measured by feasibility and cost after deletion, not only by the number of removed edges.

\section{Discussion}

The star center has four incident edges, but its neighbors are mutually nonadjacent and can share one register. A triangle vertex has only two incident edges, but its two neighbors interfere with each other, creating a three-way demand. Register pressure depends not merely on the number of conflicts but on how they interact.

The inequality $\chi(G)\leq\Delta(G)+1$ answers a worst-case sufficiency question: how many colors always suffice given only maximum degree? It does not say every graph attains the bound. In contrast, the clique lower bound is exact on chordal graphs.

The spill failure is stronger than a tie-breaking anomaly. Vertex $3$ is the unique maximum-degree choice and fails outright. A lower-degree choice succeeds, so no alternate maximum-degree choice rescues the rule. The example also separates edge reduction from chromatic reduction: four deleted star edges are less useful than two deleted triangle edges because only the latter destroy the decisive obstruction.

\section{Future directions}

Natural next steps include:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item characterize graph classes or local conditions under which maximum-degree deletion minimizes register deficit, beginning with trees, threshold graphs, and chordal subclasses;
\item optimize weighted spill cost subject to $k$-colorability;
\item connect SSA dominance and live ranges explicitly to perfect elimination orderings;
\item implement reverse-elimination greedy coloring as an optimal chordal allocator;
\item strengthen clique-based spill lower bounds; and
\item classify the smallest chordal counterexamples, including connected and weighted variants.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The eight-vertex graph $K_3\sqcup K_{1,4}$ refutes two degree-based claims about register allocation. It is chordal and has a perfect elimination ordering. Its maximum degree is $4$, yet it needs $3$ colors rather than $5$. Under a two-register budget, spilling its unique maximum-degree vertex leaves a triangle, while spilling a degree-$2$ triangle vertex yields an explicit two-coloring.

Maximum degree measures local incidence; coloring and spilling are controlled by global obstructions. For chordal interference graphs, clique number is the correct exact measure of register demand. Degree remains a useful inexpensive bound or heuristic feature, but neither exact chromatic prediction nor optimal spill selection follows from it alone.

