# When Local Busyness Misleads a Register Allocator

Register allocation turns simultaneous live ranges into a graph-coloring problem. Begin with the interactive graph below: its blue triangle is the true two-register obstruction, while its red star center merely has many mutually compatible neighbors.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## Three quantities, three roles

The chromatic number is the exact register demand; clique number is a lower bound and is exact for chordal graphs; maximum degree plus one is a general upper bound. Explore how adding harmless star leaves changes only the degree bound.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the graph definitions and counterexample proof</summary>
A proper $k$-coloring assigns one of $k$ colors to every vertex so adjacent vertices differ. In $K_3\sqcup K_{1,4}$, the triangle forces three colors and an explicit three-coloring exists, so $\chi=3$. The star center has degree $4$, hence $\Delta+1=5$. Thus $\chi
eq\max(\Delta+1,\omega)$. See the [graph coloring overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_coloring) for broader context.
</details>

## Test every spill

{{demo:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

The data isolate the mechanism: spills $0$, $1$, and $2$ hit the triangle and succeed; all other single-vertex spills leave it intact. The highest-degree candidate is therefore a failure.

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the clique lower bound</summary>
If a clique has size $m>k$, at least $m-k$ clique vertices must be spilled. Otherwise more than $k$ pairwise adjacent vertices survive. For the triangle at budget $k=2$, one triangle vertex must be removed.
</details>

## Why chordality does not rescue degree spilling

Walk through a perfect elimination ordering. This places the example firmly inside the chordal class.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the chordal coloring principle</summary>
A [chordal graph](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chordal_graph) has a perfect elimination ordering. Greedy coloring in reverse elimination order uses exactly the clique number of colors. Thus the corrected exact target for chordal interference graphs is $\chi=\omega$, while degree remains only an upper-bound parameter.
</details>

## Compute the exact demand

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal complexity notes</summary>
Exact backtracking is exponential in the number of vertices in the worst case. Checking a supplied elimination order directly takes $O(n^3)$ with an adjacency matrix, while specialized recognition is linear. Testing every one-vertex spill for two-colorability takes $O(n(n+m))$ using repeated bipartiteness checks.
</details>

## From example to allocator design

Use degree as a feature, not a certificate. A practical weighted allocator should account for spill cost, execution frequency, rematerialization, and participation in oversized cliques. Background on the compiler problem is available in this [register-allocation overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Register_allocation).

